### Step 3: Stability — The tetrahedron is the only self-stabilising polygon

**From Synergetics 223.73:**

> "Prime structure because stabilized exclusively by triangles that are
> experimentally demonstrable as being the only self-stabilizing polygons."

A triangle is rigid. A square is not — it collapses to a parallelogram
under shear. A pentagon is not — it collapses under compression. Only
the triangle is self-stabilising.

The tetrahedron is four triangles. It is the only polyhedron composed
exclusively of triangles that encloses volume with the minimum number of
vertices (4). It cannot be deformed without changing edge lengths.

```
For any perturbation δ < critical:
    Tet(ℰ + δℰ) ≈ Tet(ℰ)
    
Because:
    δVol / Vol ∝ δ²  (first-order stability)
    δAngle_sum = 0   (always 720°)
```

The 720° angle sum is invariant under ANY deformation (Syn 224.08).
This is topological — it's not a property that depends on measurement.

QED Stability.

