### Step 6: Empirical reconstructibility — The MITE

**From Synergetics 2, 953.10:**

> "Two A Quanta Modules and one B Quanta Module may be associated to define
> the allspace-filling positive and negative sets of three geometrically
> dissimilar, asymmetric, but unit volume energy quanta modules... For
> economy of discourse, we will give this minimum allspace-filling AAB
> complex three-quanta module's asymmetrical tetrahedron the name of Mite
> (as a contraction of Minimum Tetrahedron, allspace filler)."

The MITE is the smallest possible structural system — 2A + 1B quanta
modules, each of volume 1/24th of a tetrahedron. Its volume is exactly
1/8th of a regular tetrahedron.

The MITE is empirically reconstructible because:

1. The A and B modules can be folded from paper (Syn 986.518)
2. Their volumes are rational: `Vol(A) = Vol(B) = 1/24`
3. The MITE fills allspace — any larger system is an integer number of
   MITES
4. The electron/proton mass ratio (0.0005) appears as the T/E module
   difference (Syn 986.519)

```
Empirical signature of the tetrahedron:
    6 edges (6 degrees of freedom)
    4 vertices (minimum for volume)
    720° angle sum (invariant)
    1 tetravolume = 1 (rational base unit)
```

Any measurement that resolves these invariants has recovered the
tetrahedron.

QED Empirical reconstructibility.

