### Step 7: Derived uniqueness — The spectral gap at n=4

**From Synergetics 400.10:**

> "Euler's irreducible-system aspects of vertexes, areas, and edges exhibit
> the respective dissimilar quantities 4, 4, and 6 in the minimum prime
> system, the tetrahedron. This demonstrates the inherent synergy of all
> systems, since their minimum overall inventory of inherent characteristics
> is unpredicted and unpredictable by any of the parts taken separately."

APIT's trilemma (Section 4): you cannot have uniqueness + stability +
non-arbitrariness simultaneously for a continuous physical system.

Fuller's resolution: **the spectral gap at n=4 is discrete, not continuous.**

```
Candidates ordered by vertex count:
    n=1: point — no inside/outside, not a system
    n=2: line — no inside/outside, not a system  
    n=3: triangle — no volume, not a system
    n=4: TETRAHEDRON — FIRST SYSTEM (gap)
    n=5: no uniform polyhedron exists (gap)
    n=6: octahedron — not minimum (redundant)
    ...
```

The crucial point: there is NO system with 3 vertices. Three points make
a triangle — flat, no volume, no inside/outside. You must have AT LEAST
4. And with exactly 4, the only uniform solution is the tetrahedron.

```
|𝔖(ℰ)| = 1
```

because the first system is unique. Not by exclusion postulate — by
combinatorial necessity. You cannot have a system with 3.9 vertices.
You cannot have two different systems with 4 vertices — the tetrahedron
is the only 4-vertex uniform polyhedron.

```
Uniqueness is DERIVED from the discreteness of vertex counts.
Not postulated. Not excluded. DERIVED.
```

The 6-edge structure provides the 6 degrees of freedom (Syn 400.664):

> "If you want to have an instrument held in position in respect to any
> cosmic body such as Earth, it will take exactly six restraints: no more,
> no less. If we have only five restraints, then the tetrahedron will
> change its shape. Shape requires six restraints."

Six restraints, four vertices, one tetrahedron. `|𝔖(ℰ)| = 1` is not
assumed. It follows from `n=4` being the first available vertex count
for a bounded system, and from `6` being the minimum number of
restraints required to fix a shape in space.

QED Derived uniqueness.

