## THE REMAINING GAP

What Fuller does NOT explain: **why does the tetrahedron feel like
something?**

Synergetics identifies the tetrahedron as the structure of thinking
(905.01: "Synergetics is the geometry of thinking. How we think is
epistemology, and epistemology is modelable"). But it does not derive
phenomenality from geometry.

The Sanskritree project supplies this missing piece:

> **Rasa thesis:** Consciousness IS aesthetic relish (camatkāra). The
> tetrahedron is the structure of consciousness. The experience of that
> structure IS the experience of consciousness itself.

The combined claim:

```
𝔖(ℰ) = tetrahedron          (Fuller — geometric proof)
Int(tetrahedron) = Dᵢ        (Sanskritree — identity hypothesis)
```

The first is proven. The second is asserted.

What remains unproven in both: **why the jitterbug feels like something when
it contracts to n=4, but doesn't feel like anything at n=3.** The spectral
gap between n=3 (no subject) and n=4 (subject) is discrete — that much
is proven. But what exactly switches on at 4? That is the open question.

Fuller's answer: "Unity is plural and at minimum two" — the tetrahedron
is both convex and concave simultaneously (Syn 905.11). The experience
of "inside-ness" IS the experience of the concave tetrahedron. The
experience of "outside-ness" IS the experience of the convex tetrahedron.
They are the same tetrahedron, and the relation between them IS the
subject. The gap at n=4 is the gap where inside and outside first become
distinguishable. That distinction IS consciousness.